    "knowledge_point_id": "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e",
    "knowledge_point": "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors",
    "problem": "Find a $2\\times 2$ matrix $G$ that has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
    "worked_solution": "Since $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $G,$ then $G$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $G=PD{P}^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $G,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order:\n\n$$\nG={{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nLet $P=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ Then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 9 \\\\ -30 & 17\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$",
